/// mlrepro — a NULLARY generic producer's element type is undetermined (distinct from the Part C tie).
/// (iterators stress test, 2026-07-16)
///
/// CONTEXT. v-inference's Part C fix ("tie a recursive-generic producer's result element to its argument")
/// resolved the case where a producer like `from-list : List(a) -> GIter(a)` recurses through a
/// constructor, so its RESULT element ties to its ARGUMENT element — that now monomorphizes independently
/// per element type and composes at ≥2 types (verified; see
/// `mlrepro-decline-recursive-generic-producer-drops-element-tie.cdz`, now passing).
///
/// THIS IS A DIFFERENT CASE. A NULLARY generic producer — `empty : Unit -> GIter(a)` — has NO argument to
/// tie the element to, so `a` is genuinely free at every use unless the surrounding context fixes it. Two
/// symptoms, BOTH bad:
///
///   (1) DECLINE at a use that does not constrain `a`. `count(empty())` never mentions the element, so `a`
///       stays undetermined → CDZ0201 (see `count_of_empty` below). Arguably correct-to-reject (the program
///       IS ambiguous in `a`), BUT the diagnostic text blames the "result not tied to its argument" tie —
///       which is misleading here: there is no argument, and the real issue is an unconstrained nullary
///       producer, not a severed tie. A clearer message ("this nullary generic producer's element type is
///       never determined at this use — fix it by using the result where its element is constrained, or
///       annotate") would point at the actual fix.
///
///   (2) SILENT MISCOMPILE when it DOES compile. `is-empty(from-list([]))` — where `from-list([])` is the
///       nullary/unconstrained case in disguise (an empty list literal, element free) — COMPILES but then
///       misbehaves at run time (the `@test` traps rather than seeing the empty iterator as empty). A
///       nullary producer whose element defaulted to some degenerate monomorphization must not silently
///       compile to wrong runtime behavior; it should decline like (1), consistently.
///
/// EXPECTED: either (a) a single-use nullary generic producer whose element is unconstrained is REJECTED
/// with a message naming the nullary-producer cause (not the argument-tie), and (b) it is NEVER silently
/// compiled to wrong runtime behavior. Both faces should agree: unconstrained element ⇒ clean decline.
///
/// WORKAROUND in the library today (giter.cdz): avoid nullary/unconstrained-element producers — `empty()`
/// and `from-list([])` used without the element fixed elsewhere are omitted; producers that carry the
/// element (`from-list([x, ..])`, `singleton(x)`) work. A real consumer fixes `a` in context
/// (`chain(empty(), from-list([1]))`), which is the sound usage.

type GIter =
  | Nil
  | Cons(a, GIter(a))

def empty() = GIter.Nil(unit)

def from-list(xs) = match xs with
  | [] => GIter.Nil(unit)
  | [h, .. t] => GIter.Cons(h, from-list(t))

def count(it) = match it with
  | GIter.Nil(_) => 0
  | GIter.Cons(_, rest) => 1 + count(rest)

def is-empty(it) = match it with
  | GIter.Nil(_) => true
  | GIter.Cons(_, _) => false

/// FACE (1): declines CDZ0201 — element of `empty()` never determined (message blames the argument-tie).
@test
def count_of_empty() =
  if count(empty()) == 0 then unit else trap("empty count")

/// FACE (2): `from-list([])` (unconstrained element) — compiles but MISCOMPILES the empty check at runtime.
@test
def is_empty_of_empty_literal() =
  if is-empty(from-list([])) then unit else trap("empty via from-list [] should be empty")
